In PHP web security, backticks (`...`) can appear in two completely different contexts within a URL: Standard Query Parameters (Command Injection) and PHP Stream Wrappers / Protocols (Local File Inclusion leading to Remote Code Execution). [1, 2] 
The specific URL formats where backticks are utilized by attackers depend heavily on the vulnerability in the underlying backend code. [3, 4] 
------------------------------
## 1. Standard URL Query Formats (OS Command Injection)
This happens when a developer uses backticks inside a script to handle an operating system command, but appends a URL parameter straight into those backticks. [5, 6, 7] 
## Format A: Direct Parameter Append

* Target Code: $output = \ping -c 1 ` . $_GET['ip'];`
* Vulnerable URL: https://example.com
* How it is exploited: The URL passes a shell command separator (like ;, &&, or |). The backticks run the intended command, hit the separator, and seamlessly execute the attacker's trailing command (whoami) on the server. [4, 7, 8, 9] 

## Format B: Inside Quoted Fields

* Target Code: $output = \ls -la "$_GET['dir']"`;`
* Vulnerable URL: https://example.com";id;"
* How it is exploited: The attacker uses a double quote (") in the URL parameter to break out of the string boundary, appends their payload (;id;), and wraps it back up to prevent terminal syntax crashes. [10] 

## Format C: Backticks as Short Echo Tags

* Target Code: eval($_GET['code']);
* Vulnerable URL: https://example.com+\whoami`;`
* How it is exploited: If an attacker finds a script that executes raw PHP strings (via eval), they can inject backticks directly into the URL payload. PHP evaluates the backtick operator as shell_exec(), giving the attacker a rapid route to RCE. [11, 12, 13] 

------------------------------
## 2. PHP Stream Wrapper & Protocol URL Formats (LFI to RCE)
When a PHP script has a file inclusion flaw (like include($_GET['file']);), an attacker can use built-in PHP URL protocols. They use the specific shorthand backticks format (<?=\cmd`;?>)—which translates to `—to force code execution. [3, 14, 15, 16, 17] 
## Format D: The data:// Stream Wrapper

* Target Code: include($_GET['file']); (Requires allow_url_include=On in PHP configuration)
* Vulnerable URL: https://example.com
* How it is exploited: The attacker writes a payload containing backticks (<?=\cat /etc/passwd`;?>), encodes it into Base64 (PD89YGNhdCAvZXRjL3Bhc3N3ZGBhOz8+), and embeds it into the data://` URL string. The server decodes it and runs the backtick operation inside the file execution engine. [3, 5, 9, 14, 18] 

## Format E: The php://filter Chain Wrapper [19] 

* Target Code: include($_GET['file']); (Works even if allow_url_include is turned Off)
* Vulnerable URL: https://example.com|...[long chain of filters]...|convert.base64-decode/resource=/etc/passwd
* How it is exploited: This attack strings together a sequence of built-in character conversion filters. By converting dummy file bytes (like /etc/passwd) back and forth, the filter chain physically reconstructs the string payload <?=\whoami`;?>` in the server's temporary memory, triggering backtick command execution upon inclusion. [14, 20, 21, 22] 

## Format F: The expect:// Protocol Wrapper

* Target Code: include($_GET['file']); (Requires the expect extension installed)
* Vulnerable URL: https://example.com
* How it is exploited: The expect:// wrapper naturally executes everything after the protocol directly into a shell environment. While it doesn't need physical backticks in the URL, it is conceptually identical because it forces a direct pipe to the terminal. [9, 23, 24, 25, 26] 

------------------------------
## How to Tell Which One is Happening

* If the backtick payload is typed plainly to break a syntax sequence (e.g., ?cmd=ls), you are dealing with an OS Command Injection.
* If the backtick payload is wrapped inside a scheme:// protocol pattern (e.g., ?file=data://...), you are exploiting a File Inclusion flaw. [3, 4, 5, 9] 

Are you testing a server where allow_url_include is enabled, or are you looking to generate a PHP filter chain for a security authorized audit? [14] 


